\begin{abstract}
Generative structural equation models can predict distributions under intervention,
but their predictions depend on  graphs that are often uncertain. Editing that graph
can improve an  average accuracy while introducing harmful changes or consuming
computation without good repair. We study a disturbance-guided graph repair with
conditional normalizing flows and compare two procedures that reuse the same fitted
candidate models. Robust selection balances average and worst environment scores.
An anchored ensemble combines complete intervention distributions while penalizing
departure from the initial model. In a six method comparison on 15 independent
five variable systems, disturbance guided repair achieves the lowest mean
sliced Wasserstein error, with 0.106, while ensembles reduce observed harmful
conditions from eight to one out of 60 at an error of 0.130. Tests on 45 additional
systems reveal a weaker advantage as graph size increases. At 100 variables,
single graph repair retains the initial model and the ensemble improves prediction
only slightly. Two physical applications change the ranking. Ridge performs best
on a light tunnel polarizer task, whereas flexible mechanisms improve wind tunnel
intervention contrast prediction. Randomized wind tunnel measurements also expose
a missing hatch pathway and under covering predictive intervals. These findings
separate predictive usefulness from graph interpretation and show why repairs
should be evaluated against an inexpensive initial model, explicit harm criteria
and measured intervention effects.
\keywords{Structural causal models, intervention prediction, graph repair, normalizing flows, predictive ensembles}
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
Predicting a fan's response at familiar settings is different from predicting
what an external command change will do to pressure, current and noise.
Structural causal models (SCMs) address this distinction through mechanisms that remain
intact when other mechanisms are replaced \cite{pearl2009}. The practical value
depends on both the mechanisms and their produced graph. Flexible conditional densities
cannot compensate for every missing pathway produced, and a plausible graph need not
predict interventions accurately.
Given an imperfect directed acyclic graph (DAG) and the limited intervention data, which
changes deserved fittings, and question 'should they replace the initial model'?
Search can introduce harmful changes under new interventions. Average gain,
deterioration and computation therefore require separate assessment.

Our first contribution is disturbance guided repair evaluated on independent
intervention predictions and matched fitting budgets. Inverse flows reveal
variation unexplained by modeled parents. Dependence and distribution changes
in these disturbances prioritize edits, while a prediction score selects the moves.
The diagnostics are good efforts for avoiding mismatch signals, but not a proof of causal arrows.

Our second contribution compares two procedures on the same fitted candidates.
Robust selection balances average and a worst environment score changes. Anchored
ensembling combines intervention distributions with a penalty for departing from
the initial model. Neither procedure requires additional mechanism fitting.
Our third contribution connects controlled comparisons to larger graphs,
contrasting topologies and two physical applications. Diagnostic repair leads
on small systems, while ensembling trades accuracy for fewer harms. At 100
nodes, active single graph repair disappears. In a wind tunnel, randomized
pressure responses expose a missing hatch pathway. The findings distinguish
predictive benefit from structural interpretation.






\section{Background and Related Work}
\subsection{Mechanisms and structural information}
In the Pearl framework, each structural equation generates a variable from its
parents and an exogenous disturbance. An intervention replaces the assigned
mechanism while other mechanisms remain shared \cite{pearl2009}. We use structural
equation models in this generative sense, not as covariance exclusive 'latent factor'
models. Causal interpretation requires assumptions about omitted causes,
intervention targets and mechanism stability.

Normalizing flows provide invertible density transformations with tractable
Jacobians \cite{papamakarios2021}. Spline flows offer flexible scalar maps with
analytic inversion \cite{durkan2019}. Deep structural causal models use such
mechanisms for exogenous noise inference and causal queries \cite{pawlowski2020},
while causal normalizing flows study their architectural and causal consistency
\cite{javaloy2023}. We adopt these components with exact parent masks and examine
the consequences of uncertainty in the masks.

Interventions can refine the structural distinctions available from observational
data \cite{hauser2012}. Continuous structure learning introduces differentiable
acyclicity constraints \cite{zheng2018}. Differentiable Causal Discovery from Interventional Data (DCDI) shares intact
neural mechanisms and includes a flow variant
\cite{brouillard2020}. Our bounded discovery adaptation is followed by common flow
refitting. Unlike repair, it receives no imperfect prior graph. Residual
independence and environmental invariance also have established roles in causal
inference \cite{peters2014,peters2016}. Our adaptive diagnostic priorities do not
inherit those methods' identification or confidence guarantees.

\subsection{Priors and predictive reliability}
Causal Modelling Agents combine language model graph proposals, deep structural
models and iterative amendment \cite{abdulaal2024}. ABAPC-LLM integrates fallible
semantic constraints with available statistical evidence and studies memorization controls
\cite{li2026}. CauScientist, a preprint, already combines hybrid initialization,
proposed edits and intervention aware verification \cite{peng2026}. Combining
language models with generative mechanisms and graph verification is therefore
not our novelty claim. We evaluate a continuous data adaptation and a semantic
prior as supplementary comparisons.

Worst case risk criteria motivates the protection beyond average performance
\cite{hashimoto2018}. Our mean/max rule uses a finite calibration set, rather than
a distributional uncertainty set. Predictive stacking motivates combining entire
distributions \cite{yao2018}. We fit a small mixture with the energy score
\cite{gneiting2007} and an explicit initial model penalty. This is neither
the Bayesian 'leave one out' stacking nor a posterior over causal graphs. The penalty
also changes the strictly proper score's optimization target.

The gap is the practical consequence of repair under finite data and compute.
Recorded interventions from Causal Chambers \cite{gamella2025} allow us to test
prediction, harm and graph interpretation on equipment as well as synthetic
systems. We develop and compare repair procedures without claiming a new
identification theorem or invention of their established components.
\section{Methods}
\subsection{Generative mechanisms and interventions}
Let $G$ be a directed acyclic graph on $d$ observed variables, with parent set
$\pa_G(j)$ for variable $j$. We represent each mechanism by a conditional
invertible map $T_{\theta_j}$ with parameters $\theta_j$,
\begin{equation}
 X_j=T_{\theta_j}\bigl(U_j;X_{\pa_G(j)}\bigr),
 \qquad U_j\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1),\qquad U_1,\ldots,U_d\ \text{independent}.
 \label{eq:scm}
\end{equation}
Exact parent masks and topological evaluation define generation. Acyclicity,
causal sufficiency and stable intact mechanisms hold in controlled systems. On
equipment, they are predictive modeling approximations.

Each scalar map consists of two rational quadratic splines followed by a conditional
shift and positive scale. Conditional log density is the 'standard normal' log
density of the inverse disturbance plus the inverse log Jacobian. An intervention
on target set $I_e$ replaces those mechanisms with externally assigned values
$a_{I_e}$. It does not condition the original target mechanism on some unusual
observation. For environment $e$, let $x_{ei}$ be the recorded row $i$ and $n_e$ its
sample size. The prediction density and node-balanced fitting criterion are:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 p_G^e(x_{\bar I_e}\mid a_{I_e})
   &=\prod_{j\notin I_e}p_{\theta_j}(x_j\mid x_{\pa_G(j)}),
       \qquad x_{I_e}=a_{I_e},\\
 \ell_{ej}(G)&=-\frac{1}{n_e}\sum_{i=1}^{n_e}
       \log p_{\theta_j}(x_{eij}\mid x_{ei,\pa_G(j)}),\\
 \NLL_G(\mathcal E)&=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{j:m_j>0}\frac{1}{m_j}
       \sum_{e\in\mathcal E:j\notin I_e}\ell_{ej}(G),
 \quad m_j=\sum_{e\in\mathcal E}\mathbf{1}\{j\notin I_e\}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:fit}
\end{equation}
Here $\bar I_e$ denotes nontarget variables and $\mathcal E$ the partition's
environments. Stochastic assignments follow the specified joint law they have.
Uniform sampling of eligible environments and their rows estimates each node's
loss. Fits use Adam weight decay $10^{-5}$ and separate early stop observations.
Diagnostics do not modify this loss.
Canonical parent set caching makes each fit independent of the search route.
An edit refits only mechanisms with changed parents. All others remain intact.
Grouped GPU execution keeps separate parameters and stopping states.

\subsection{Disturbance priorities and the candidate bank}
We infer disturbances on search observations and ask where the fitted mechanisms
appear inconsistent with their assumptions. The priority of node $j$ combines
three descriptive quantities,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \widehat U_j&=T_{\theta_j}^{-1}(X_j;X_{\pa_G(j)}),\\
 D_j&=\overline{z\{\operatorname{MMD}^2(\widehat U_j^e,\widehat U_j^0)\}}
 +\overline{z\{\operatorname{HSIC}(\widehat U_j^e,X_{\pa_G(j)}^e)\}}\\
 &\quad+\overline{z\{\operatorname{HSIC}(\widehat U_j^e,\widehat U_k^e)\}}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:diagnostics}
\end{equation}
Maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) compares a disturbance's distribution with a
reference environment. The Hilbert-Schmidt independence criterion (HSIC) measures
dependence on parents or other intact disturbances \cite{gretton2012,gretton2007}.
Bars denote averages over available environments and, for the last term, eligible
peers $k\ne j$. Each statistic is centered and scaled by its row permutation
reference distribution, then truncated at zero. The scaling denominator adds
$10^{-6}$ to the permutation standard deviation. These scores are neither
$p$ values nor probabilities that an edge is correct. Serial dependence further
limits their inferential interpretation on physical data.
Legal additions, deletions and reversals are ranked by summed priorities of
affected mechanisms, with shuffled ties and every fourth proposal random.
Rounds evaluate up to three unseen graphs under common acyclicity, root and
indegree constraints. A move requires score improvement $\epsilon=0.005$.

The common score combines the balanced negative log likelihood, intervention
distribution error and edge count. To support robust selection, we retain an
exact environment decomposition. With $K$ intervention environments and shared
noninterventional contribution $O_G$, it is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 S(G)&=\NLL_G(\mathcal E)+\frac{\beta}{K}\sum_{e=1}^K W_e(G)
                      +\lambda\frac{|E(G)|}{d},\\
 S_e(G)&=O_G+\frac{K}{d}\sum_{j\notin I_e}\frac{\ell_{ej}(G)}{m_j}
                +\beta W_e(G)+\lambda\frac{|E(G)|}{d},
 \qquad S(G)=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{e=1}^K S_e(G).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
$E(G)$ is the edge set, $\beta=1$, $\lambda=0.02$, and $W_e$ is sliced-Wasserstein
error on standardized nontarget outcomes, defined below. Search and calibration
use 512 generated observations and 64 projections per environment. The shared
term is the contribution of all noninterventional environments to
Eq.~\ref{eq:fit}, not their unweighted average. In the wind tunnel there is no
unmanipulated environment, $O_G=0$, and all command densities are omitted while
the denominator remains the full $d=11$.

The bank contains $G_0$ and every fitted candidate, including rejected search
moves. It is frozen before calibration, audit or test access
(Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}). Random repair builds its own bank with the same
operations and limits, changing only proposal priority.

