\subsection{Average and robust selection}
This average score repair selects from the bank using the mean calibration score,
requiring an improvement of $\epsilon$ relative to $G_0$. It does not simply return
the search incumbent. Robust selection compares each calibration environment
with the same environment under $G_0$,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \Delta_e(G)&=S_e(G)-S_e(G_0),\\
 R_\rho(G)&=(1-\rho)\frac{1}{K}\sum_{e=1}^K\Delta_e(G)
                    +\rho\max_e\Delta_e(G),\qquad \rho=0.5.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:robust}
\end{equation}
The minimum-scoring candidate replaces $G_0$ only if $R_\rho\le-\epsilon$.
Otherwise it retains $G_0$. A $\rho=0$ control reproduces average selection.
Equation~\ref{eq:score} preserves the original objective weights. The worst
calibration score does not bound error under unseen interventions.

\subsection{Anchored predictive ensembling}
We shortlist $G_0$ and at most two other distinct graphs with the best search
scores. Calibration does not determine the shortlist. For components
$G_0,\ldots,G_{L-1}$, one task-specific weight vector is then learned and frozen
for every final setting and target,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 P_w^e&=\sum_{k=0}^{L-1}w_k P_{G_k}^e,
           \qquad w_k\ge0,\quad \sum_k w_k=1,\quad L\le3,\\
 \widehat w&=\arg\min_w\left\{
       \frac{1}{K}\sum_e\widehat{\operatorname{ES}}_e(P_w)
                   +\tau(1-w_0)^2\right\},\quad \tau=0.01,\\
 \operatorname{ES}_e(P,y)&=\frac{\E\|Z-y\|_2-\tfrac12\E\|Z-Z'\|_2}
                                      {\sqrt{d-|I_e|}},
       \qquad Z,Z'\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}P,\\
 J(w)&=w^{\mathsf T}a-\tfrac12 w^{\mathsf T}Bw+\tau(1-w_0)^2.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:mixture}
\end{equation}
Distances use training standardized nontarget outcomes. Vector $a$ and matrix
$B$ contain a component to observation and a component to component mean distances,
equally weighted across environments. Fixed 512-sample component draws permit
deterministic optimization, checked against a development simplex grid of step
$0.005$. Unpenalized and uniform mixtures are supplementary controls.

The generation draws one component per-complete observation and samples its
intervened SCM. Graphs are not selected separately by node or averaged into an
adjacency matrix, and weights combine predictions but not causal posterior beliefs.

\subsection{A common audit}
After calibration, each of the procedure will receive one comparison with fixed-$G_0$ flows
on a separate audit partition. It passes only if its mean intervention distance
is at most $1.05W_0+0.001$ and its normalized negative log likelihood is at most
the initial value plus $0.05$. Rejection returns $G_0$ without trying another
candidate. Audit likelihood averages the truncated joint negative log density
divided by the number of intact variables within each environment, then averages
environments equally. This differs deliberately from the node balanced search
normalization. For a mixture, component joint densities are combined with
\texttt{logsumexp} before this normalization. Averaging these nodewise mixture densities
would describe a different model.

\subsection{Prediction and harmful changes}
The synthetic primary endpoint is a joint non-target sliced-Wasserstein error at
new intervention settings. For empirical distributions $P,Q$ on $q$ standardized
outcomes, unit projection directions $v_r$ and quantile functions $F^{-1}$, the
executed approximation and harm indicator are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 W(P,Q)&=\frac{1}{RN_q}\sum_{r=1}^{R}\sum_{b=1}^{N_q}
   \left|F^{-1}_{v_r^{\mathsf T}P}(t_b)-F^{-1}_{v_r^{\mathsf T}Q}(t_b)\right|,
       \quad t_b=\frac{b-\tfrac12}{N_q},\\
 R&=256,\qquad N_q=512,\qquad
 E_2(G)=\frac{1}{K_2}\sum_{e\in\mathrm{T2}}W_e(G),\\
 H(G)&=\mathbf{1}\left\{E_2(G)-E_2(G_0)>
                              \max\bigl(0.001,0.05E_2(G_0)\bigr)\right\}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:evaluation}
\end{equation}
Here $K_2$ counts final T2 environments. The projections are normalized Gaussian
directions with frozen seeds. Harm refers to a returned repair relative to the
same fitted initial model. Its severity is the positive error increase, reported
among the 'harmful conditions' as well as across all conditions. It is not assigned
to methods that do not repair a graph. We also report marginal error, effect
error, worst regime error, predictive coverage and, in synthetic systems,
descendant specific errors. None is interchangeable with graph recovery.

The wind tunnel primary endpoint instead targets measured actuator effects. For
target $t$, acquisition $r$ and engineering outcome $j$, let $\widehat\delta_{trj}$
be the generated high from -low mean and $\delta_{trj}$ the recorded contrast.
With fitting standard deviation $s_j$, it is
\begin{equation}
 C=\frac{1}{3}\sum_{t=1}^{3}\frac{1}{|\mathcal R_t|}
       \sum_{r\in\mathcal R_t}\frac{1}{5}\sum_{j\in\mathcal O}
       \frac{|\widehat\delta_{trj}-\delta_{trj}|}{s_j},
 \qquad \delta_{trj}=\overline Y_{trj,\mathrm{high}}-
                         \overline Y_{trj,\mathrm{low}}.
\label{eq:contrast}
\end{equation}
$\mathcal R_t$ contains the reserved acquisitions for that target, and $\mathcal O$
contains two current and three pressure outcomes. Nuisance commands are fixed
within each randomized contrast. This error is not numerically comparable to
the synthetic sliced Wasserstein error. Wind tunnel harm remains a secondary
distributional check using the threshold in Eq.~\ref{eq:evaluation}, applied per
reserved acquisition relative to fixed flows.


\section{Experimental Design}
\subsection{Methods and information available}
Independent empirical marginals (M0) use an empty graph and predict no downstream
effects. Their continuous density likelihood is unavailable. Ridge (M1) fits
linear Gaussian mechanisms on the same initial graph $G_0$ supplied to repair,
with an unpenalized intercept and residual variance floor $10^{-4}$. Separate
development data select regularization from $\{0.001,0.01,0.1,1\}$, giving $0.1$
for synthetic systems and $1$ for the wind tunnel.

DCDI-DSF plus common flows (M2) discovers a graph without $G_0$, then refits the common
spline mechanisms. Its official deep sigmoidal flow density is used with a bounded
CUDA optimization adaptation. Thresholding and removal of the weakest offending
edges enforce acyclicity and the common indegree limit. We distinguish valid
projected predictions from optimizer convergence and from native DCDI.

Diagnostic repair (M3), robust selection (M4) and the anchored ensemble (M5) share
one fitted bank. Fixed-$G_0$ flows isolate mechanism flexibility. Random priority
repair changes the scheduling policy while retaining legal edits and limits.
Oracle flows use the true graph only on a prespecified subset. Synthetic truth
is restricted to generation, controlled prior corruption, oracle fitting and
isolated evaluation, never ordinary selection or language-model prompts.


\subsection{Controlled systems and structural complexity}
The principal comparison contains fifteen independent five-node SCMs, five each
with linear, nonlinear additive and nonlinear heteroscedastic mechanisms. Two
intervention budgets and two priors give sixty matched conditions. Nonlinear
mechanisms include bounded parent effects and interactions. Corruption levels
$c=0.2,0.5$ request $\max(1,\operatorname{round}(c|E|))$ legal edits, cycling through
addition, deletion and reversal in randomized order. These are edit counts,
not guaranteed fractions of incorrect edges. Actual structural Hamming distance (SHD) is recorded. Ridge, fixed flows and
repair receive this imperfect prior.

Scaling uses thirty independent SCMs across 20, 50 and 100 nodes, five additive
and five heteroscedastic systems per size. Two priors give sixty conditions.
Weakly connected sparse graphs have $2d-3$ edges. Mechanisms combine signed smooth
parent functions, bounded interactions and positive parent dependent noise
scales. Degree normalization prevents numerical explosion. A frozen numerical
rejection rule set excluded no primary system aspect.
Fifteen additional 50 node heteroscedastic SCMs examine dense, hub and deep
profiles. Each has five systems and two priors. Hub and deep graphs have 97
edges. Deep graphs have 49 edge longest paths but limited unlabeled topological
diversity. Dense graphs have 322 edges and mean indegree 6.44. Common indegree
limits of three for sparse/deep, six for hubs and eight for dense graphs admit
the truth. Randomized labels conceal generating topological order.

Fitting, early stopping, search, calibration and audit have observational counts
4,096, 1,024, 1,024, 512 and 512. Only fitting observations define standardization.
Five node systems have three visible intervention targets, each with $B=100$ or
$400$ nontest observations allocated 50\%, 10\%, 20\%, 10\% and 10\% across
these roles. All roles count toward $3B$. Scaling holds visible target coverage
at 20\% with $B=400$, giving 1,600, 4,000 and 8,000 intervention observations.
This controls coverage, not total experimental data. Targets are selected
independently of graph and method outcomes.
Familiar assignments are normal with standardized mean $+1$ and standard
deviation $0.25$. T1 uses fresh observations at those settings. Primary T2 changes
the mean to $-1$. T3 uses unseen targets at mean $+1$, two targets in five node
systems and another 20\% of nodes in scaling. Each final environment uses 2,048
observations and the same number of generated predictions per method. Repeated
budgets, priors and targets do not increase independent-SCM counts. Development
systems and final systems have separate seeds.



\subsection{Two physical applications}
Both applications use Causal Chambers measurements \cite{gamella2025}. The
light tunnel task models the color  red, green and blue commands, current, a polarizer
command and its angle readout from \nolinkurl{lt_interventions_standard_v1}.
It tests a reserved stronger polarizer regime with firmware quantized setpoints,
not transmitted light intensity. Sensor setting interventions are excluded here.
Separated within acquisition segments with guard gaps supply the non-test roles,
using 6,472 reference policy and 1,600 targeted rows. Each final regime is one
acquisition. This is an exploratory application on a familiar apparatus, not
independent physical replication.

The wind tunnel study asks how fan duty and hatch opening affect pressure,
speed and current. We audited \nolinkurl{wt_walks_v1} and
\nolinkurl{wt_validate_v1}, using actual measurements and protocols. The model
contains eleven quantities: two fan duty fractions, hatch angle, two speeds in
rpm, two currents in amperes, three pressures relative to ambient in pascals,
and ambient pressure. Pressure is not airflow. Sensor reference voltages are not
motor supply voltages, so current is not converted to power. Microphone
oversampling differs between training and validation, precluding a compatible
joint acoustic model. Separate microphone circuit voltages are not calibrated
decibels.

Development used 14,000 recorded rows. Speed-settling estimates ranged from
2.8 to 10.3 seconds. A frozen history filter, startup exclusion and three-second
spacing select quasi-steady observations. Residual pressure autocorrelations of
0.80 to 0.88 make the static SCM a predictive surrogate, not an identified
instantaneous DAG. Eight trajectory files supply fitting data, with disjoint
files for other roles. Their 3,399 non-test observations include one
preprocessing-only row. Files share an acquisition sequence, not independent
days. Joint command assignments and sensor settings are preserved. Commands and
ambient pressure are declared roots. Gaussian model selection by the Bayesian information criterion initializes
$G_0$ from fitting rows, without the reference graph. All command mechanisms are replaced at prediction.

The primary wind test has 699 measurements in five randomized acquisitions,
with ten-second waits. Each fan changes from duty $0.01$ to $1$ with the other
fan at $0.01$ and hatch closed. Hatch opening changes from $0^\circ$ to $45^\circ$
with inlet/outlet duties $1/0.01$. Each fan has two acquisitions, the hatch one.
Assignments are verified against protocols and actual commands. This tests new
runs, settings and assignment distributions on one apparatus, with all targets
already visible in training. Generation uses 4,096 observations per arm. Three
shorter wait acquisitions are secondary.

Five separate optical SCMs test coherent, anonymous and shuffled semantic
metadata. A frozen Qwen3-8B \cite{yang2025} supplies actual graph proposals and
legal-edit choices with the common verifier. A separate wind experiment compares
semantic and fitting  only starts. Prompts exclude final measurements and reference
graphs. Public benchmark knowledge during pretraining remains possible.

\subsection{Resources and statistical analysis}
Neural fitting, discovery and generation use one RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell with
32,623 MiB memory. Language and flow stages run separately. The Appendix gives
software, architecture and optimization details.

Five node search permits twelve candidates with two stalled rounds. Scaling
compares this prefix with caps of 24, 36 and 72 that continue after stalls.
Each large discovery fit and bank has a 3,600-second ceiling. Wind banks permit
24 graphs, 48,000 updates and 1,800 seconds. Each repair method pays the full
bank cost plus selection, audit and prediction, despite shared execution.
Discovery and refitting costs are separate. Matched repair comparisons use
completed prefixes, reporting achieved work.

Synthetic 95\% intervals use 2,000 family stratified SCM cluster resamples,
retaining all paired conditions. Paired sign randomization tests use Holm
correction \cite{holm1979} over fifteen main method pairs for five-node systems
and 24 declared contrasts for scaling. They assume exchangeability of paired
signs under the null. Zero observed harms do not imply zero population risk.

Light tunnel intervals use 200 resamples of contiguous ten row reporting blocks.
Wind intervals use 2,000 hierarchical resamples of acquisitions within actuator
and ten-row blocks within acquisition. They condition on one apparatus, fitted
models and fixed Monte Carlo samples. File exchangeability is approximate and
there is only one hatch acquisition. Five grouped fitting file refits separately
describe selection sensitivity. Four wind tunnel acquisition sign flip comparisons
use Holm correction. These empirical intervals differ from predictive intervals
for individual outcomes and do not capture all causal or calibration uncertainty.


\section{Results}
\subsection{Small systems favor diagnostic repair on average}
Diagnostic repair achieves the lowest mean T2 error, 0.106
(Table~\ref{tab:controlled}), with a gain of 0.0757 over fixed flows. It differs
from each other main method after correction over fifteen pairs. Its paired
difference from the robust selection is $-0.0114$, with SCM-cluster interval
$[-0.0220,-0.0027]$ and adjusted $p=0.0469$. The difference from the ensemble is
$-0.0240$, with interval $[-0.0379,-0.0116]$ and adjusted $p=0.0082$. This supports
the average advantage within the tested matrix. Figure~\ref{fig:controlled}
separates mechanism families and the repair-risk tradeoff.

