the executed adaptation and information budget, not all converged native DCDI
implementations.
Matched-update and time-limited comparisons also favor diagnostic repair over
random priorities (Appendix).

\subsection{Ensembling changes the accuracy and harm tradeoff}
The ensemble has one harmful condition among sixty, versus eight for diagnostic,
seven for robust and five for random repair. Its maximum deterioration is 0.0104 versus 0.0770 for diagnostic and robust selection. The ensemble-minus-
diagnostic harm-frequency difference is $-0.1167$, with descriptive interval
$[-0.2000,-0.0500]$. Removing the anchor yields error 0.1113 and eight harms.
Uniform weights yield 0.1222 and six harms. Anchoring trades average accuracy
for fewer observed deteriorations. 

Robust selection retains $G_0$ in 38.3\% of conditions versus 25.0\% for average
selection, yet avoids only one additional harm. Its mean worst-regime error is
0.1702 versus 0.1484. Protecting calibration's worst score does not improve this
held-out endpoint. Ensemble retention is 1.7\%, where any nonzero alternative
weight counts as a mixture.

