\subsection{The physical applications favor different models}
Ridge leads on the light-tunnel polarizer task, scoring 0.0856 with conditional
interval $[0.0737,0.1141]$. Diagnostic/robust repair score 0.1349, the ensemble
0.1460, fixed flows 0.1461 and discovery plus flows 0.1723.
Figure~\ref{fig:physical} compares accuracy and cost within each application.
Their different endpoints are not pooled.

On the wind tunnel, fixed flows reduce standardized contrast error from ridge's
0.8131 to 0.3602 on the same initial graph (Table~\ref{tab:wind}). Diagnostic
repair improves it to 0.3310, nearly tied with random repair at 0.3320. Robust
selection scores 0.3680 and the ensemble 0.3506. Thus most of the gain over
ridge is already available through flexible mechanisms without repair.

The capped and projected DCDI-DSF adaptation with common flows has the best
observed aggregate contrast error, 0.2199. It reaches the 40,000-step cap with
raw acyclicity divided by $d$ of $1.86\times10^{-6}$ and removes thirteen edges
during projection. Discovery takes 671 seconds and common-flow refitting 28
seconds. This aggregate result does not imply superiority for every outcome.
Its upwind-pressure contrast error is 4.97 Pa versus 3.44 Pa for fixed flows,
while its downwind-pressure error is 1.39 Pa versus 5.30 Pa. No prespecified
formal test compares its overall lead with all competitors.

