\subsection{A process graph checked against measured effects}
Diagnostic repair adds two edges and removes one from the fourteen-edge initial
graph (Fig.~\ref{fig:process}). Five of fifteen selected edges occur in fewer
than three of five grouped refits. Neither added edge recurs. This is conditional
selection sensitivity, not a causal probability.

Randomized contrasts provide a different form of evidence. With outlet duty
$0.01$ and hatch closed, raising inlet duty from $0.01$ to $1$ increases upwind
relative pressure by 26.51 Pa, with block interval $[25.97,26.94]$, and inlet
current by 0.1060 A, with interval $[0.1030,0.1089]$, in the prespecified inlet
acquisition. In the prespecified outlet acquisition, raising outlet duty with inlet duty
$0.01$ and hatch closed reduces
downwind relative pressure by 26.15 Pa, with interval $[25.91,26.49]$, and
increases outlet current by 0.1079 A, with interval $[0.1039,0.1128]$. These
measurements support actuator total effects at the stated settings. They do
not distinguish all direct from mediated pathways in the fitted DAG.

