The most informative discrepancy is the hatch. At inlet duty $1$ and outlet
duty $0.01$, opening it reduces upwind relative pressure by 5.43 Pa, with
interval $[4.52,6.38]$, and downwind relative pressure by 7.61 Pa, with interval
$[6.71,8.58]$. Yet the initial and selected diagnostic graphs leave the hatch
disconnected. Their missing response is visible in Fig.~\ref{fig:responses}.
An engineer relying only on this graph could overlook a measured means of
changing pressure. The randomized result supports a total hatch effect, without
identifying which direct arrow should be inserted.

Inlet current changes by $-0.00244$ A. Its interval includes zero
($-0.00521$ to $0.00048$ A), providing no evidence of a saving. The ensemble also
offers no single repaired explanation: its initial component carries weight
0.915 and the other retained component 0.085.  A separate compatible acoustic acquisition gives
an outlet-command microphone-circuit change of 0.461 V, with interval
$[0.259,0.657]$. Because training sensor settings differ, this is a measured
response rather than validation of a generative acoustic model.

