The offline decision exercise also separates capabilities. A development-fixed
upwind-pressure threshold of 6.1875 Pa defines feasibility. Each method chooses
between the two recorded arms, minimizing predicted summed current when feasible.
All methods choose the same low arms in all five acquisitions. Their observed
mean-pressure violation count is zero and mean current regret is 0.000421 A.
The hatch comparison drives this regret, but its summed-current contrast interval
includes zero. This exercise establishes no method advantage, verified optimal
control or energy saving. More densely recorded alternatives would be needed
to support such decisions.

\subsection{Semantic information does not establish an advantage}
The functioning language-model comparator generates 122 valid edits, with 97
score rejections and 25 accepted search moves. A complete-graph versus single-edit
contract that admitted no edits is excluded as interface-failure evidence,
rather than interpreted as failure of language-model refinement.

On the wind tunnel, semantic initialization worsens every matched primary
comparison, increasing diagnostic error from 0.3310 to 0.5020. Five semantic
SCMs and anonymous/shuffled controls also fail to demonstrate a predictive
benefit (Appendix). This bounds the tested prior and interface, not engineering
knowledge in general.



\section{Discussion}
The results distinguish prediction, graph recovery, uncertainty and operating
decisions. Useful fan predictions coexist with a missing hatch pathway,
undercoverage and identical operating choices. Success in one capability does
not establish the others.

Disturbance priorities improve average prediction under a limited fitting budget,
but audited repair can still deteriorate on new interventions. Anchored ensembling
adapts predictive combination \cite{gneiting2007,yao2018} by retaining initial-model
weight, trading some accuracy for fewer observed harms. Robust selection's weaker
result shows that protecting the worst calibration score need not protect new
regimes.

At scale, a fixed acceptance tolerance acts on a score averaged over more nodes
and environments while diagnostics remain subsampled. Local improvements may
become harder to detect, and candidate counts remain small relative to initial
structural error. These explanations are untested. Scale-adjusted acceptance, different moves
and richer diagnostics remain future work. At 100 nodes, fixed flows match single-graph repair at about one tenth of
its charged cost. Ensemble generation takes 19.96 seconds versus 0.62 seconds
for diagnostic repair with identical sample counts. Shared-device timings include
worker contention and are not isolated hardware benchmarks.

Five systems per mechanism or topology cell limit replication. Physical evidence
comes from single apparatuses with serial dependence. Shared ambient measurement
error, omitted temperature and fluid pathways challenge independent disturbances.
Sparse support limits extrapolation, and semantic priors may reflect benchmark
knowledge. None establishes identification or universal protection from harm.

The most useful additional wind measurements would be randomized fan/hatch grids,
repeated on separate days with common sensor settings. Longer response traces
would support temporal models. Calibrated motor voltage and acoustics are needed
for power and sound-pressure conclusions. Denser recorded alternatives would also
make operating-decision comparisons informative.



\section{Conclusion}
Disturbance-guided repair improves small-system average intervention prediction.
Anchoring reduces observed harms at some accuracy cost. Larger graphs expose
little active repair, and physical applications favor different methods.
Randomized wind-tunnel measurements reveal a missing pathway in a useful
predictive graph. Repair therefore requires independent assessment of
prediction, deterioration, computation and structural interpretation.

