\subsection*{Implementation and resource details}
Spline mechanisms use eight bins, linear tails beyond magnitude five and two
width-64 SiLU hidden layers. The positive final scale is $0.05$ plus a softplus
output. Adam uses learning rate $0.001$, batch size 256 and coupled weight decay
$10^{-5}$. The latter adds the gradient of
$\tfrac12 10^{-5}\|\theta_j\|_2^2$ during fitting, but is not added to the graph
score. Early likelihood is checked every fifty updates, with patience five and
improvement tolerance $10^{-5}$. Maximum updates are 1,000 per mechanism in
synthetic comparisons and 800 in the wind tunnel. The best early-stop checkpoint
is reused. Parent-set keys include data roles, preprocessing, ordered parents,
architecture, optimizer and implementation identity.

Diagnostics use Gaussian kernels averaged across bandwidths $0.5,1,2$ for
disturbances. The parent kernel bandwidth is the square root of the number of
parents. The five-node comparison uses at most 256 rows and 32 permutations.
Scaling and wind-tunnel diagnostics use 64 rows, 16 permutations, up to eight
peers and four additional intervention environments. All environments still
enter search and selection scores. The wind-tunnel reference is the first
fixed-order search environment because there is no observational environment.
Command densities are excluded from diagnostics, scores and audits. M0 uses
observational fitting marginals in synthetic tasks and eligible acquisition-policy
marginals in the wind tunnel. Auxiliary command-root fits reuse fitting rows
with all physical-node losses masked, and are always replaced at generation.

DCDI-DSF uses two width-64 leaky-ReLU hidden layers and two sigmoidal-flow
layers of width eight. The adapted augmented-Lagrangian optimizer uses RMSprop
at learning rate $0.001$, batch size 256 and adjacency threshold $0.5$.
The controlled and scaling step cap is 60,000, versus 40,000 for the wind tunnel.
Known perfect-intervention targets mask their density terms. No convergence
claim follows merely from obtaining an acyclic projected graph.

DCDI regularization is $0.1\sum A_{ij}/d^2$ for weighted adjacency $A$.
Let $h(A)=\operatorname{tr}(\exp A)-d$. Its optimization tolerance uses
$h(A)/N\le10^{-8}$ and a thresholded acyclic graph. Here $N=\max(1,h(A_{\mathrm{allow}}))$,
where $A_{\mathrm{allow}}$ is the binary matrix of permitted edges. The scaling and wind-tunnel convergence audit additionally requires
$h(A)/d\le10^{-8}$. These criteria are different. In the five-node controlled
records, the 29 normalized-stopping successes have raw $h(A)$ approximately
$4.5\times10^{-7}$ to $5.1\times10^{-7}$, so they do not meet the raw-per-node
criterion. Projection remains explicit even when the optimizer stops.

The principal small-system comparison consumed 5.77 device-job hours, scaling
and topology experiments 19.48 hours, and the wind-tunnel application 0.79 hours,
including development, pilots and unsuccessful attempts. The independent
search-selection evaluation consumed 10.32 hours. These device totals account
for overlapping work on one GPU and differ from summed standalone logical costs.
Three workers shared the GPU for the small comparison and six for scaling.
The largest recorded process allocation during scaling was 1.72 GiB. Device-wide
sampling every fifteen seconds observed a 7,840 MiB peak, which is not a continuous
peak measurement. Wind-tunnel language-model inference used 15.48 GiB allocated
memory and about 69 seconds including loading and generation.

Five-node matched-time analysis counts canonical fitting. Scaling additionally
counts search and diagnostics. Light-tunnel charges include the initial graph's
scoring cost, while wind-tunnel charges exclude untimed common initialization
and preprocessing. Cross-application time ratios therefore have no direct
interpretation. CUDA arithmetic and gradients were verified with driver
580.159.04 and PyTorch 2.8.0 using CUDA 12.8.

At a 6,000-update ceiling, diagnostic/random errors are 0.1191/0.1529 with
5,574/5,383 achieved updates. At a 120-second fitting ceiling, errors are
0.1430/0.1577 and both achieve about 111.4 seconds. These completed prefixes
support a scheduling benefit at tested ceilings, without equating total search
overhead. Ridge takes only about 0.04 seconds to fit.

A separate search-selection evaluation on thirty five- or ten-node SCMs found
diagnostic/fixed errors of 0.0993/0.1774, but ten of 120 conditions remained harmful.
Diagnostic repair worsened its semantic and physical predictions and used more
updates than random repair. Those results are preserved separately because the
selection protocol lacks the calibration refinement used in the principal
comparison.

